---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<!-- Master copy — the live file is mmp-site/src/pages/kids.astro. Edit here, copy in, build, push only that file. -->
<Layout title="Kids" description="Saved kids activities from Instagram, with instructions you can actually use — plus the conflict-resolution playbook for when kids fight.">
  <main class="bg-[#0c0b09] min-h-screen px-4 py-14 sm:py-20 text-[#ece5d3]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.35em] text-[#c9a86a]">Saved, distilled</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-[#f7f0dc]">Kids</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] max-w-2xl">
        Activities saved to the kids collection on Instagram, written up with
        instructions so you don't have to re-watch the reel to run them —
        plus the conflict-resolution stuff, so it's all in one place.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">Summaries, not transcripts. Every activity links to the original reel. Checked daily; new saves are added here.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Activities</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6">

        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">Screen-free · Toddlers</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Floating spoon drawing</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">From <a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/mom.in.a.million" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@mom.in.a.million</a> — day 8 of a 30-day screen-free activities series.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">A drawing that lifts off a spoon and floats on water. It genuinely stops toddlers in their tracks — and needs almost nothing.</p>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">You need</h4>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>A metal spoon</li>
            <li>A dry-erase marker</li>
            <li>Cold water in a bowl or tray</li>
            <li>Paper, if you want to lift the print off</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">Instructions</h4>
          <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Draw on the back of the spoon with the dry-erase marker.</li>
            <li>Slowly dip the spoon into the cold water.</li>
            <li>Watch the drawing float off the spoon onto the water's surface.</li>
            <li>Lay a piece of paper on top to transfer the print, like a tattoo.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]"><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Why it works:</strong> dry-erase ink doesn't bond to metal the way permanent ink does, so water getting underneath lifts it right off.</p>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DeuTsiGJ3qM/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch the original reel →</a></p>
        </article>

        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">Indoor · Burn energy</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Top 5 indoor energy-burning activities</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">From <a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/craftmonsterz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@craftmonsterz</a> — counted down from 5 to 1.</p>
          <ol class="mt-5 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]" reversed>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">5. Tape Jump:</strong> lay down play cushions and stick masking tape on the wall. Jump between two pieces of tape, or over one piece — then move the tape higher after every round and see how high you can go before you break it.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">4. Treasure Rescue:</strong> put a pool noodle down and a bunch of stuffed animals behind it. One adult guards the “treasure” while the kids sneak or run around to grab a stuffed animal.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">3. Stuffie Knockdown:</strong> simple aim and coordination practice — knock the stuffed animals down, with giggles built in.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">2. Clear the Room:</strong> put a long piece of cardboard in the middle of the room with a hole cut in the middle. Set a timer for one minute; each team throws balls through the hole to clear their side. No throwing over the box. Fewest balls left on your side wins.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">1. Hula Hoop Jump:</strong> the all-time favourite in this house — dead simple, and it keeps kids entertained and burning energy the longest.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWyzOVmCdAw/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch the original reel →</a></p>
        </article>

      </div>

      <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">When kids fight: conflict resolution</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] max-w-2xl">
        The short version of the sibling-fighting page, kept here so the kids
        page is self-contained. The full page has the scripts, the disagreements,
        and the red flags.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6">
        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-[#c9a86a]/30 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <h3 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">The 60-second playbook</h3>
          <ul class="mt-4 list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Pause first:</strong> stop touching, move apart, breathe, check in — then resume. Start at 15 seconds, build to 30, then a minute. It's a scaffold for self-regulation, not a punishment.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Coach, don't referee:</strong> don't take sides or hand down a verdict. Narrate what you see, validate it, then hand the problem back: “What are you guys going to do about it?” Conflict is where kids learn to disagree, share power, and repair.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Toddlers can't self-resolve:</strong> stop the hurt first, help both kids regulate, and only then teach the missing skill once calm — e.g. “You wanted the toy. Try: ‘Can I have a turn?’”</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Fair ≠ equal:</strong> “We don't do fair. We do individual needs.” A protest is often really “Do I matter as much?” — name what the other child needed, hold the boundary, and let disappointment exist.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Step in for safety, not verdicts:</strong> separate and create space when bodies or feelings are unsafe. The full page covers where rivalry ends and bullying begins.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="/parenting/sibling-fighting">Full sibling-fighting page, with scripts and sources →</a></p>
          <p class="mt-2"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="/parenting">All parenting topics →</a></p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-14 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">Source: @matthewjmiller07 Instagram saved collection “kids”, last checked 29 Oct 2026 (2 items). This page is a personal summary project, not medical or psychological advice.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
